  "nl_text": "The schemabench_0030 database is designed to manage user authentication and push notification devices. At its core, the database contains a table called auth_user, which stores information about each user, including their unique identifier, password, last login date, and various status flags such as whether they are a superuser, staff member, or active. The auth_user table has a primary key on the id column, ensuring that each user is uniquely identified. The push_notifications_apnsdevice, push_notifications_gcmdevice, push_notifications_webpushdevice, and push_notifications_wnsdevice tables store information about different types of push notification devices, including their names, active status, creation dates, and registration IDs. Each of these tables has a foreign key referencing the id column in the auth_user table, establishing a relationship between a user and their associated devices. The push_notifications_apnsdevice table also has a device_id column, which is a character string, and a registration_id column, which is a string of up to 255 characters, while the push_notifications_gcmdevice table has a device_id column with a larger capacity and a registration_id column that can store much longer strings. The push_notifications_webpushdevice table includes additional columns for p256dh, auth, browser, and registration_id, which are all strings, while the push_notifications_wnsdevice table has a device_id column and a registration_id column with a large capacity. All of these tables have a primary key on the id column, ensuring that each device is uniquely identified.",
  "gold_question": "Background / Context\n\nThe project involves developing a mobile push notification system using Django. This system allows sending push notifications to mobile and web devices through multiple channels, including APNS (Apple Push Notification Service), FCM/GCM (Firebase Cloud Messaging/Google Cloud Messaging), WNS (Windows Notification Service), and WebPush. The system utilizes core Device models to manage and send notifications.\n\nObjectives\n\nThe primary objective is to enable efficient delivery of push notifications to a variety of devices. The system should facilitate sending both single and bulk notifications, handle device registrations, and update existing devices based on their registration IDs.\n\nStakeholders & Users\n\n- End Users: Individuals receiving notifications on their devices.\n- Administrators: Manage devices and notifications via the admin panel.\n- Developers: Implement and maintain the system within Django applications.\n\nFunctional Descriptions\n\n- Device Models: Implement models for different notification services (GCMDevice, APNSDevice, WNSDevice, WebPushDevice) with key attributes like name, active status, user linkage, device ID, and registration ID.\n- Notification Sending: Support sending notifications through various services either to individual devices or in bulk.\n- Admin Panel: Provide an interface to manage devices and facilitate testing of notifications, including a feature to handle duplicate registration IDs by updating existing records.\n\nNon-Functional Descriptions\n\n- Scalability: System must efficiently manage and send notifications to a potentially large number of devices.\n- Maintainability: Clean and adaptable code structure to allow easy updates and maintenance.\n- Performance: Ensure quick delivery of notifications with minimal delay for end users.\n- Security: Secure handling of user data and notifications, including proper management of user authentication and registration IDs.",
  "input_json": "/data/SchemaBench/Experiment/schema2text_llama31/0030.json",
  "ddl": "-- Schema for database: schemabench_0030\nCREATE TABLE `auth_user` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `password` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `last_login` datetime DEFAULT NULL,\n  `is_superuser` tinyint(1) NOT NULL DEFAULT '0',\n  `is_staff` tinyint(1) NOT NULL DEFAULT '0',\n  `is_active` tinyint(1) NOT NULL DEFAULT '1',\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `push_notifications_apnsdevice` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `device_id` char(36) NOT NULL,\n  `registration_id` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `active` tinyint(1) NOT NULL DEFAULT '1',\n  `created_at` datetime NOT NULL DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP,\n  `user_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `push_notifications_apnsdevice_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`user_id`) REFERENCES `auth_user` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `push_notifications_gcmdevice` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `device_id` varchar(512) NOT NULL,\n  `registration_id` text NOT NULL,\n  `active` tinyint(1) NOT NULL DEFAULT '1',\n  `created_at` datetime NOT NULL DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP,\n  `user_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `push_notifications_gcmdevice_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`user_id`) REFERENCES `auth_user` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `push_notifications_webpushdevice` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `device_id` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `p256dh` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `auth` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `browser` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `registration_id` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `active` tinyint(1) NOT NULL DEFAULT '1',\n  `created_at` datetime NOT NULL DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP,\n  `user_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `push_notifications_webpushdevice_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`user_id`) REFERENCES `auth_user` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `push_notifications_wnsdevice` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `device_id` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `registration_id` text NOT NULL,\n  `active` tinyint(1) NOT NULL DEFAULT '1',\n  `created_at` datetime NOT NULL DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP,\n  `user_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `push_notifications_wnsdevice_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`user_id`) REFERENCES `auth_user` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;",
